  <data name="Tutorial_P0_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Welcome to RemEx</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P0_Body" xml:space="preserve"><value>RemEx turns your PC into a remote command center. Monitor hardware sensors, launch apps, manage processes, and stream your desktop — all controllable from your Android device.

Your PC runs the RemEx Host, which your phone connects to over your local network.</value></data>

  <data name="Tutorial_P1_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>How It Works</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P1_Body1" xml:space="preserve"><value>This desktop app includes an embedded RemEx Host that starts automatically. Your Android phone connects to it — not the other way around.</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P1_Body2" xml:space="preserve"><value>For always-on access (even when you're not logged in), you can install the host as a Windows service or Linux daemon. This lets your phone connect anytime.</value></data>

  <data name="Tutorial_P2_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Run as a Windows Service</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P2_Body1" xml:space="preserve"><value>To keep RemEx running 24/7 without being logged in, install it as a Windows service:</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P2_Steps" xml:space="preserve"><value>1. Open Settings (⚙) → scroll to 'Windows Service'
2. Set your Windows username and password
3. Click 'Install as Service'
4. The service starts automatically on boot</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P2_Note" xml:space="preserve"><value>The service runs under your user account so it can access your desktop session for screen capture and app launching.</value></data>

  <data name="Tutorial_P3_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Linux Service Setup</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P3_Body1" xml:space="preserve"><value>On Linux, RemEx Host can run as a systemd service for automatic startup.</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P3_Steps" xml:space="preserve"><value>sudo cp remex-host.service /etc/systemd/system/
sudo systemctl enable remex-host
sudo systemctl start remex-host</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P3_Link" xml:space="preserve"><value>For detailed instructions, see docs/linux-service-setup.md on GitHub</value></data>

  <data name="Tutorial_P4_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>HWInfo Integration (Recommended)</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P4_Body1" xml:space="preserve"><value>For detailed hardware monitoring (300+ sensors), install HWiNFO64 — it's free for personal use.</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P4_Steps" xml:space="preserve"><value>1. Download from hwinfo.com
2. Run HWInfo → Settings (⚙) → Main Settings
3. Check 'Shared Memory Support' → OK
4. HWInfo must be running for RemEx to read its sensors</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P4_Warning" xml:space="preserve"><value>⚠ HWInfo's Shared Memory auto-disables after 12 hours (free version). Re-enable it periodically, or consider the Pro license.</value></data>

  <data name="Tutorial_P5_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sensor Monitoring</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P5_Body1" xml:space="preserve"><value>RemEx supports two telemetry sources:</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P5_HWInfo" xml:space="preserve"><value>🟢 HWInfo: 300+ sensors — CPU temps, GPU clocks, fan speeds, voltages, power draw. Requires HWInfo running.</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P5_BuiltIn" xml:space="preserve"><value>🪟 Built-in: Basic sensors — CPU usage, RAM, disk I/O, network rates. Always available, no extra software.</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P5_Body2" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pin your favorite sensors to the Home screen from Settings → Pinned Sensors.</value></data>

  <data name="Tutorial_P6_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>App Launcher Setup</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P6_Body1" xml:space="preserve"><value>The App Launcher lets you start programs on your PC from your phone. Apps must be configured here first.</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P6_Steps" xml:space="preserve"><value>1. Navigate to the Launcher page (sidebar)
2. Click '+ Add Program'
3. Browse to the .exe or shortcut
4. Launch from your Android app!</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P6_Note" xml:space="preserve"><value>This works even with the standalone Remex.Host service — configure apps in the desktop client at least once.</value></data>

  <data name="Tutorial_P7_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connecting Your Phone</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P7_Body1" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your Android app finds this PC automatically using mDNS. Both devices must be on the same Wi-Fi / LAN.</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P7_Body2" xml:space="preserve"><value>If auto-discovery doesn't work, find your PC's IP manually:</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P7_IpSteps" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows: CMD → ipconfig → 'IPv4 Address'
Linux: ip addr  or  hostname -I</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P7_Body3" xml:space="preserve"><value>Enter ws://&lt;IP&gt;:5005/ws in the Android app's connection settings.</value></data>

  <data name="Tutorial_P8_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>You're All Set!</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P8_Body1" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use the sidebar to navigate between Home, Sensors, Remote Control, App Launcher, Task Manager, and Remote Desktop.</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P8_Body2" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Settings (⚙) to customise your theme, connection, stream quality, and pin sensors to Home.</value></data>
  <data name="Tutorial_P8_Replay" xml:space="preserve"><value>You can replay this tutorial anytime from Settings.</value></data>

